.TH KILL 1
.SH NAME
kill \- send a signal to programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kill
.RB [ \-INT | \-TERM | \-KILL | \-HUP | \-USR1 | \-USR2 | \-\fIn\fP ]
.IR pid ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kill
is a command built into the shell,
.BR sh (1).
It sends a signal to each process
.I pid
(see
.BR ps (1)),
.B SIGTERM
unless another is named. Unless the program catches or ignores it, the
signal ends it;
.B SIGKILL
always does. A user may signal only their own processes; root any but the
supervisor,
.BR init (8).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-INT
SIGINT (2), what Ctrl-C sends
.TP
.B \-TERM
SIGTERM (15), the default
.TP
.B \-KILL
SIGKILL (9): cannot be caught
.TP
.B \-HUP
SIGHUP (1), what a terminal that goes away sends
.TP
.BR \-USR1 ", " \-USR2
SIGUSR1 (10), SIGUSR2 (12)
.TP
.BI \- n
the signal numbered
.I n
(1 to 31)
.SH EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 if a signal could not be sent.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR kill (2),
.BR sh (1)
.SH BUGS
Process groups
.RI ( \-pid )
cannot be named here.
